y show Talarico narrowly ahead, prediction markets price him around 60-64%, and several raters call the race Lean D, though others still see a Tossup. Paxton's vulnerability to opposition spending and Talarico's money edge support a small Democratic lead, but the state's Republican lean and Talarico's recent campaign-trail absence argue for keeping uncertainty wide.", "watch": "Whether Talarico's return to the trail holds his polling edge through early voting, and whether Paxton can consolidate Republicans after a bruising primary.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: John Cornyn (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Texas's open Senate seat has become one of the cycle's most competitive races. Attorney General Ken Paxton won the Republican nomination after defeating incumbent Senator John Cornyn, while state Representative James Talarico carries the Democratic banner. Recent polls from mid-September through early October show Talarico leading by roughly three points on average, with surveys from YouGov and the New York Times/Siena showing him up five to six points among likely voters.\n\nTalarico has dramatically outraised Paxton and benefits from heavy outside spending attacking Paxton, whose legal and ethical controversies have long made him a polarizing figure. Prediction markets give Talarico about a 60-64% chance of winning. Still, Texas's Republican-leaning electorate keeps the race close, and Paxton's path runs through turning out low-propensity voters in a midterm environment that currently favors Democrats.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6697, "margin": 2.08}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6924, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7255, "margin": 2.8}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6997, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6523, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6444, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6983, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.